    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d269-a1">

                <pb facs="0408" n="292" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg292"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1—Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">
                        Horacio Vásquez to Chargé Johnson.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moca</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 26, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Minister</hi>: Your telegram received. I
                    believe that not by means of threats but through tried and respected
                    institutions will the country obtain a lasting peace. The threats and conduct of
                    Minister Sullivan in support of the indefensible administration of Bordas
                    contributed hot a little to one of our most disastrous wars. Moreover, I do not
                    believe that President Wilson has the right, under any pretext, to violate the
                    sovereignty of an independent people. I am pledged to the cause of peace and
                    shall remain unalterably firm, as I said to Senator Castro in my published
                    telegram; but I believe that respect for personal liberty and for the laws by
                    the Government is indispensable in order to obtain the so greatly desired
                    end.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Horacio Vásquez.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
